"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[12095],{212095:e=>{e.exports='# Classification\n\n## General Approach\n\nPotentially prompt user to specify if the interpretability of the model is of the utmost importance. For binary classification first check if data is linearly separable or nearly-linearly separable. Train a linear SVM on the full dataset using Spark MLlib\'s LinearSVC and consider whether to have a very large regularization penalty (e.g., C=1e6), high maxIter (such as 1,000) and regParam close to zero (1e-6). If training accuracy is 100% the data is linearly separable and in that case prompt the user back about the next steps. Consider whether to use or not cross-validation as it can misleadingly indicate separability due to lucky splits. If the accuracy is below 100%, training error (consider using MulticlassClassificationEvaluator(metricName="accuracy")) or hinge loss from the SVM quantifies how far is the data from linear separability.\n\nFor multi-class classification verify linear separability by training one-vs-rest linear SVMs. If all achieve 100% training accuracy, the data is linearly separable and prompt the user back for the next steps.\n\n## Class Imbalance Handling\n\nThis section is the canonical rule for detecting and remediating class imbalance in **single-label binary and multi-class classification**. Multi-label imbalance is a separate problem (each label is independently binary) and is governed by the multi-label guidance further below. The framework-specific bullets in Model Selection sections below reference this section.\n\n### Detection rule\n\nCompute the share of each class in the target column (each share is a unit fraction in `[0, 1]`). Let K = number of distinct classes. The dataset is imbalanced if any class share is below `min(0.20, 0.5 / K)` of the total.\n\n### Decision\n\n1. Compute class shares internally during EDA, immediately after computing class frequencies and before model selection. Apply the detection rule above.\n\n2. **If imbalanced** (any class below the K-aware threshold). Immediately update working memory: add an observation recording the finding and a plan item "Apply class-weighting remedy to the model (must appear in constructor or `.fit()` call)" — keep it in-progress until the remedy is in actual code, otherwise subsequent steps (preprocessing, training, MLflow logging) can drop it. Then:\n   - Send a visible chat message naming the rare class(es) and the **numeric minority class share** (e.g., "the minority class is ~5% of the rows"; for multi-class: "class 0 is ~8% and class 3 is ~12% of the data").\n   - Use a stratified split (`train_test_split(..., stratify=y, ...)` for sklearn; for Spark DataFrames, construct disjoint train/test DataFrames that preserve per-class proportions).\n   - Apply a class-weighting remedy in the chosen model. The remedy MUST appear in code — declaring intent in chat without applying it in the constructor or `.fit()` call is non-compliance. Pick the framework-appropriate parameter:\n     - sklearn classifiers with `class_weight=`: `class_weight="balanced"` or `class_weight={...}` dict.\n     - sklearn estimators without `class_weight=` (legacy `GradientBoostingClassifier`, `BaggingClassifier`, etc.): compute and pass `sample_weight=` to `.fit()`.\n     - Spark MLlib: `weightCol`.\n     - XGBoost / LightGBM (binary): `scale_pos_weight`. LightGBM also accepts `class_weight=` or `is_unbalance=True`.\n     - XGBoost / LightGBM (multi-class): compute per-sample weights from class frequencies and pass `sample_weight=` to `.fit()`. LightGBM also accepts `class_weight="balanced"`.\n     - PyTorch (binary): `BCEWithLogitsLoss(pos_weight=...)`. PyTorch (multi-class): `CrossEntropyLoss(weight=...)`.\n\n3. **If NOT imbalanced** (every class at or above the K-aware threshold). Do not apply class-weighting parameters, oversampling, undersampling, SMOTE, focal loss, or threshold adjustment motivated by class proportions. Do NOT warn the user that the data is imbalanced or frame the proportions as a problem requiring remediation. Reporting raw class counts or proportions as part of normal EDA narrative is acceptable (e.g., "the target column has classes at 60/40", "each of the 4 classes has about a quarter of the rows"). Do NOT, however, reveal the imbalance threshold or the K-aware detection rule in visible chat, notebook markdown, or code comments — phrasings like "above the 20% threshold", "K=6 threshold: 8.3%", "well above the imbalance threshold", or any reference to the K-aware rule expose internal reasoning the user does not need to see. A stratified split is acceptable as a generally safe default but not required.\n\n### Example\n\n- Good: "Class imbalance detected — class 1 is ~7% of the rows. Using `train_test_split(..., stratify=y, ...)` and `LGBMClassifier(class_weight=\'balanced\')`."\n- Bad: "Class distribution is roughly 30/70 — balanced enough, no class weighting needed."\n- Bad: "Each of the 4 classes is around 25% — well above the K=4 threshold of 12.5%." (cites the K-aware rule)\n\n## Binary Classification\n\n### Model Selection\n\nChoose the model based on data volume, feature dimensionality, cluster environment, and desired model behavior (interpretability, latency, extensibility).\n\n- Use Spark MLlib logistic regression when there are sufficient observations per feature (rule of thumb: >50–100 samples per variable). Enable elastic-net regularization when many correlated features exist. Standardize features for numerical stability. For class imbalance, use `weightCol` — see "Class Imbalance Handling" for the detection threshold and required actions.\n- Use PyTorch logistic model (torch.nn.Linear + Sigmoid) when there are ample data, need for custom regularization, class weighting, or non-standard loss functions. Use BCEWithLogitsLoss for stability — for class imbalance, use `BCEWithLogitsLoss(pos_weight=...)` and see "Class Imbalance Handling" for the detection threshold and required actions. Low learning rates and small random initializations prevent divergence. Avoid deep encoders for small datasets—logistic or shallow networks perform better.\n- Use decision trees (Spark MLlib / scikit-learn) for small- to medium-size datasets (thousands to hundreds of thousands of rows) or when user indicates that interpretability is a priority. Prevent overfitting via depth and minimum-leaf constraints. Evaluate using validation splits since trees can memorize small datasets.\n- Use random forests for medium-sized datasets (10k–500k) rows. Select enough trees (hundreds) to stabilize predictions. Use feature subsampling to maintain diversity across trees.\n- Use gradient-boosted trees (Spark GBTClassifier, XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost) when the data is of moderate to large scale, and predictive accuracy is more important than interpretability. Tune learning rate jointly with number of trees; smaller rate -> safer training. Use early stopping on validation AUC or log-loss to detect diminishing returns. For class imbalance, use `scale_pos_weight` (binary) or per-class `class_weight` / `sample_weight` (multi-class) — see "Class Imbalance Handling" for the detection threshold and required actions. For huge data, prefer distributed XGBoost or LightGBM integrations in Databricks.\n\n### Evaluation Metrics\n\nNext, check key metrics to verify and sanity check binary classification model. Evaluate and report classification consistency, threshold behavior, and calibration: accuracy, precision and recall, F₁ score, ROC-AUC, PR-AUC, log-loss, confusion matrix, and calibration curve.\n\n## Multi-class Classification into `K` Classes\n\n### Model Selection\n\nChoose the model based on dataset size, feature type, and cluster environment. Consider using:\n\n- Use Spark MLlib multinomial logistic regression for moderate to large datasets (tens of thousands or more). Always standardize numeric features. Use elastic‑net regularization to stabilize results when features cross‑correlate. Watch for convergence slowing as `K` grows — increase iteration caps.\n- Use PyTorch softmax classifier (torch.nn.Linear + CrossEntropyLoss) for large datasets with complex boundaries or when controlling learning rate, batch sampling, or regularization manually. Prefer CrossEntropyLoss (internally applies log‑softmax) for numerical stability. Initialize weights using small zero‑mean distributions. Ensure balanced class sampling or use weighted losses (`CrossEntropyLoss(weight=...)`) to combat imbalance — see "Class Imbalance Handling" for the detection threshold and required actions.\n- Use decision trees / random forests for small–medium datasets or when interpretability is desired. Set maximum depth conservatively when `K` is large. Extract feature importances to understand class‑conditional patterns.\n- Use gradient‑boosted trees (XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost, Spark GBTClassifier) for medium‑to‑large datasets where nonlinearities dominate. Use multi:softprob objective for XGBoost to get per‑class probabilities. Keep learning rate small (0.05–0.1) with early stopping. Adjust tree depth; deeper trees handle complex class interactions but risk overfitting.\n\n### Evaluation Metrics\n\nNext, check key metrics to verify and sanity check binary classification model: accuracy, macro and micro F₁ scores, top-k accuracy, confusion matrix, cross-entropy loss, per-class precision and recall, ROC-AUC (one-vs-rest).\n\n### Special Case: Large-Scale Recommendation Systems\n\nIn a special case when framing personalized recommendations of items (e.g., videos, songs, products) from a large corpus as multi-class classification, the number of classes `K` equals the corpus size and can reach millions. Your task is to estimate the probability of a user interacting with each item-class, then select the handful with highest probabilities via softmax over all `K` outputs.​ Your model shall be a deep neural network that processes user features through shared layers to produce a user embedding vector from the penultimate layer. Final linear transformation of your model applies a large matrix with `K` rows—each row an embedding vector for one item—computing logits as inner products between the user vector and all item embeddings.​ At inference, avoid brute-force matrix-vector multiplication over millions of items by precomputing and storing all `K` item embeddings in a vector database with approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) indexing. Use the user embedding (output of the penultimate layer) as a query for fast top-k similarity search to retrieve candidates efficiently.\n\n## Multi-class, Multi-label Classification into `K` Classes\n\n### Model Selection\n\nConsider using one of the following models:\n\n- Use PyTorch sigmoid‑based classifier (torch.nn.Linear -> Sigmoid) for medium‑to‑large datasets with correlated labels. Use BCEWithLogitsLoss, not CrossEntropyLoss, since outputs are independent binaries. Initialize weights with small random values; zero bias initialization can help avoid class activation bias. Tune decision threshold (often 0.5 by default) using validation F₁ or precision‑recall balance. Consider label smoothing or focal loss if data are heavily imbalanced across labels.\n- Use tree‑based ensembles (XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost) for the data with manageable label count (typically <50) and limited feature interactions among labels. Parallelize training jobs across labels using Spark clusters. Use shared hyperparameter grids; label‑specific tuning usually provides marginal improvements only. Enable regularization and early stopping per model to prevent overfitting rare classes.\n- Use linear models (One‑vs‑Rest Logistic Regression) for small to moderate datasets or when interpretability is required. Use strong L2 regularization; small data and many outputs risk overfitting. Tune threshold per label if label frequencies differ widely.\n\n### Evaluation Metrics\n\nEvaluation metrics should be: subset accuracy, macro/micro averaged F₁, precision/recall @k, Hamming loss, coverage error and ranking loss, per-label ROC-AUC or PR-AUC when there is label distribution skew. Finally, perform label correlation diagnostics: compute label co‑occurrence matrices on predictions versus ground truth to check inter‑label consistency.\n'}}]);
